0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.10",{"topic":{"id":"4.10","title":"Digital Evidence Presentation Techniques","minutes":11,"summary":"Make complex evidence understandable with accurate timelines, relationship diagrams, and carefully contextualized excerpts.","sections":[{"title":"Choose a visual for the question","body":["A timeline communicates sequence; a relationship diagram communicates links; a table communicates exact values and sources. Choose the simplest form that answers the question. Label time zones, uncertainty, inferred relationships, and source identifiers. Avoid decorative graphics that imply certainty or causation where only association was observed.","For a payment-fraud case, place the original instruction, account-change request, authentication event, and transfer on one carefully sourced timeline. Show missing intervals rather than drawing an uninterrupted line through them. A visual should make the evidence easier to inspect, not replace the underlying records."]},{"title":"Excerpts, redaction, and context","body":["A short chat excerpt can be readable but misleading if surrounding messages are omitted. Identify the excerpt, provide the full source under controlled access, and explain selection criteria. Redact unrelated personal information in presentation copies while preserving the original. Mark transformations so the audience does not confuse a demonstrative aid with the evidence itself.","Hash tables should identify exactly which file or output each value covers. Screenshots should include enough context to locate the source, but not expose passwords or unrelated data. For printed material, use legible type, page numbers, exhibit references, and captions that remain understandable in black and white."]},{"title":"Explain without overwhelming","body":["Introduce the question, then the key finding, then the supporting artifacts. Define technical terms in ordinary language. Use an analogy only after stating its limits: a hash is like a fingerprint for comparison, but it does not identify a human sender. Invite the audience to inspect the source references.","Check every visual against the report before presentation. An attractive diagram with an unsupported edge can create a false impression. The course’s printable notes preserve source links and explanations so learners can review the reasoning away from the interactive interface."]}],"practice":{"title":"Design a one-page evidence timeline","scenario":"You have four events with known times and one uncertain attribution.","steps":["Place the known events in time order with sources.","Mark the uncertain attribution explicitly.","Choose a table or timeline that stays readable in print.","Add a caption explaining what the visual does not prove."],"debrief":"A good presentation makes uncertainty visible and traceable rather than hiding it behind visual polish."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa"],"sources":["bsa","cert"],"labId":"certificate","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain choose a visual for the question and apply it to an investigation.","Explain excerpts, redaction, and context and apply it to an investigation.","Explain explain without overwhelming and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.10","prompt":"What should a relationship diagram distinguish?","options":["Only colours, with no labels.","Observed links, inferred links, and their sources.","The examiner’s favorite hypothesis only.","The price of each forensic tool."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Labelled relationships and source references let the audience evaluate what the diagram actually establishes.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
